 "purpose": "A red-leather volume of nine literary manuscripts and five letters. Held for the letters: to Clegg, to Douglas, to Kersley and to Lane, plus Clegg's own letter to Wilde. The Kersley and Lane letters are candidates for records of ours that are unplaced or privately held - see README.md.",
 "downloaded": "2026-08-10",
 "download_url": "https://www.themorgan.org/collection/Oscar-Wilde",
 "rights_note": "Not stated per-item by the Morgan; recorded as UND. These are kept private, outside web/, like the rest of this tree.",
 "notes": [
  "Per-page `file` is relative to `../../manuscripts/<key>/`, the private tree that holds the images. The path is metadata; the images are what stays unpublished.",
  "No IIIF service, so `iiif_url` is empty and this archive cannot back a `facsimile` record; it is a private reference copy only.",
  "record_url takes `{page}`, not `{pointer}`: the Morgan paginates this facsimile 1..50 and publishes no per-image token, so the page number is what addresses a sheet. The two are different fields on purpose - `pointer` holds the Morgan's own file name (wilde_ma7258.01-1), which encodes the item and the side within the volume rather than the reading position, and is worth keeping for that. Templating it into the record url would build a broken link, which is what this manifest used to say to do.",
  "Fetched by hand in a browser: themorgan.org returns 403 to any non-browser request, and Chrome gates script-triggered downloads after the first per page load.",
  "`letters` on the item records what each page range holds, which record cites it, and - where nothing does - why it is worth keeping anyway. A bound volume needs this at page-range level; the HRC manifests answer the coarser question with `matches` at item level.",
  "A `matches` entry carries only structured facts: `pages`, `what`, `printed` {work, locator} and, where there is no printed pointer, `person`. Prose about why an entry matters lives in this archive's README, not here - the manifest restates neither the connection nor the commentary."
 ],
